---
import Layout from "../components/Layout.astro";
import {
  STORED_WHEN_ACCOUNTS_SHIP,
  K_ANON_PUBLISH_FLOOR,
} from "../lib/storageDisclosure";
---
<Layout title="Privacy — Civic Ledger">
  <h1 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Privacy</h1>
  <p class="mt-2 max-w-prose text-neutral-700">
    What happens to you when you read this site. The first half of this page is
    what is true today. The second half is what the site will store once it has
    accounts and sends alerts — written now, before any of it is built, so the
    description comes first and the collection second.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-8 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">What this site is</h2>
  <ul class="mt-2 max-w-prose list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm text-neutral-700">
    <li>Static pages, built from a dataset the operator maintains. No account, no login.</li>
    <li>
      The site is delivered by Cloudflare. Like any company that delivers a
      website, Cloudflare receives the requests your browser makes and can
      count them. That is a record of your reading, and it is outside the
      operator's control.
    </li>
    <li>
      Reading the record needs no account, and it never will. Every bill,
      topic and member also has a feed you can follow in a feed reader without
      signing up for anything.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="mt-8 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">The one promise</h2>
  <p class="mt-1 max-w-prose text-sm text-neutral-700">
    Your data is never sold. That is the whole of it, and it is deliberately
    the only thing on this page offered as a promise rather than a
    description. Features planned for this site need to keep a record of what
    you follow, so a pledge not to collect anything would be one we could not
    keep — and a promise that cannot be kept is worse than no promise. This
    one holds whatever gets built, because selling your data would be a
    decision by the operator rather than something the software could do on
    its own.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-8 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">What is stored today</h2>
  <p class="mt-1 max-w-prose text-sm text-neutral-700">
    Every fact on a member, vote, or bill page links to the original government
    record it came from, so you can check it yourself. The dataset and source
    code are kept in a repository that is not currently public.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-8 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">What will be stored when accounts ship</h2>
  <p class="mt-1 max-w-prose text-sm text-neutral-700">
    Accounts, email alerts and profiles are planned, not built. None of this
    exists yet and nothing below is being collected today. When they arrive,
    the site will hold these things about you, and
    <strong>the operator can read all of them</strong>:
  </p>
  <ul class="mt-2 max-w-prose list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm text-neutral-700">
    {STORED_WHEN_ACCOUNTS_SHIP.map((thing) => <li>{thing.text}</li>)}
  </ul>
  <p class="mt-2 max-w-prose text-sm text-neutral-700">
    This is a description of what the features need, not a guarantee that
    nothing else is ever kept. If that list grows, this page is rewritten
    before the thing that grew it ships.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-8 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">Your profile is the part other people can see</h2>
  <p class="mt-1 max-w-prose text-sm text-neutral-700">
    Everything else in that list is between you and the operator. A profile is
    the exception, and it is off until you turn it on: there is an explicit
    publish step, and <strong>anything you choose to publish on your profile
    is public</strong> — readable by anyone, and by anything that crawls the
    web. Nothing reaches a profile by default.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-8 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">Deleting your account</h2>
  <p class="mt-1 max-w-prose text-sm text-neutral-700">
    Deletion is real deletion. Asking for your account to be deleted removes
    its rows across every one of the things listed above, rather than flagging
    them as hidden. Leaving an alert list is separate and simpler: every email
    carries an unsubscribe link that works without logging in.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-8 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">Who else sees it</h2>
  <p class="mt-1 max-w-prose text-sm text-neutral-700">
    Cloudflare, the same company that delivers this site, is also the company
    that will send the email. It therefore receives your email address and the
    text of every message sent to you, and it keeps its own records of that,
    which can be demanded from it directly. Email is the only alert channel
    being built. A phone number, and the phone company that would carry the
    message, only come into it if text alerts are ever added — and that is not
    decided.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-8 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">Numbers published about readers</h2>
  <p class="mt-1 max-w-prose text-sm text-neutral-700">
    If this site ever publishes a figure drawn from what readers do — how many
    people follow a bill, say — that figure has to cover at least
    {K_ANON_PUBLISH_FLOOR} people. Below that it is not published at all, because a
    small enough group is a group someone can be picked out of. The default is
    to publish nothing.
  </p>

  <p class="mt-8 max-w-prose text-sm text-neutral-700">
    The <a href="/threat-model" class="underline hover:text-neutral-900">threat model</a>
    is the long version of this page: what someone could learn about you, what
    a legal demand would produce, and — stated plainly — what is no longer
    promised.
  </p>
</Layout>
